{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How does regional political climate moderate the relationship between political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake. We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions. A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster (OR =.84, p = .049). Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate. Observed patterns linking liberal political climates with vaccine uptake among conservative individuals have important implications for vaccination efforts among conservative individuals in the United States.[page 1]"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate significantly moderates the relationship between political conservatism and both vaccine and booster hesitancy, such that conservatives in liberal political climates are less hesitant and more likely to receive boosters than conservatives in conservative climates, while liberals are consistently less hesitant and more likely to receive boosters regardless of climate.",
      "Conservative individuals are more likely to receive booster doses in liberal regions, indicating that environmental factors such as political climate can influence vaccine behavior in politically conservative individuals."
    ],
    "evidence": [
      "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions. A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster (OR =.84, p = .049). Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate. Observed patterns linking liberal political climates with vaccine uptake among conservative individuals have important implications for vaccination efforts among conservative individuals in the United States.[page 1]",
      "Regression analysis (Table 3) indicated that when controlling for age, education, income, race, and vaccine access, political conservatism and conservative regional climate were positively associated with vaccine hesitancy (β = .23, p < .001; β = .11, p = .001; respectively). Additionally (Table 4), the interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate was significant (β = .09, p = .003)(Table 3, Fig 2). As shown in Table 3, political conservatism and conservative regional climate were also positively associated with booster hesitancy (β = .18, p < .001; β = .11, p = .002; respectively) and the interaction between these two variables with booster hesitancy was significant (β = .09, p = .003)(Table 4, Fig 2). As shown in Table 5, political conservatism and conservative regional climate were negatively associated with vaccine status [odds ratio (OR) =.68, p < .001; OR =.72, p = .004; respectively]. The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833). As shown in Table 5, political conservatism and conservative regional climate were negatively associated with booster status (OR =.82, p = .022; OR =.77, p = .004; respectively). Additionally, there was a significant interaction (Table 6, Fig 2) between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (OR =.83, p = .026). Results for all regression models with control variables can be found in Tables A-D in S4 File.[page 6]",
      "For booster status, the interaction between political orientation and political climate indicated that the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).[page 8]"
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          "However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.[page 1]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood of receiving a booster was associated with their political climate.",
        "quotes": [
          "Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.[page 1]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The most politically liberal individuals were over 65% likely to get a booster regardless of regional climate, while the most conservative individuals in liberal climates were 60% likely to get a booster, and those in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get one.",
        "quotes": [
          "For booster status, the interaction between political orientation and political climate indicated that the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).[page 8]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate was significant for booster hesitancy and booster status, with a significant odds ratio of 0.83 (p = .026) indicating lower booster uptake among conservatives in conservative climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "Additionally, there was a significant interaction (Table 6, Fig 2) between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (OR =.83, p = .026).[page 8]"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors. While our analyses controlled for demographic covariates commonly associated with vaccine hesitancy, we did not formally assess effect modification by these factors. Consequently, the reported pooled associations should be interpreted as average effects across the sample, and we cannot exclude the possibility that heterogeneity across demographic subgroups could produce aggregation effects such as Simpson’s paradox.[page 10]",
      "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability and recall bias, and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status.[page 10]",
      "Our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes participants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.[page 10]",
      "This data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.[page 10]"
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      "The authors place themselves within the framework of political psychology and public health research, extending existing literature by integrating both individual-level political orientation and regional political climate as moderating variables in vaccine behavior. They extend prior studies that focused on either individual ideology or regional climate by examining their interaction, arguing that the interplay between personal and environmental factors is critical in understanding vaccine hesitancy and uptake.[page 1]"
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      "The paper attributes to prior research the claim that 'conservative political orientation and a conservative political climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 vaccine' and then disputes or builds on this by showing that the effect is moderated by regional climate, particularly that conservatives in liberal climates are less hesitant and more likely to receive boosters.[page 10]"
    ]
  }
